What quantization data types are supported by the BitsAndBytes library?

BitsAndBytes Quantization Types

The BitsAndBytes library mainly supports 2 quantization levels, but within those it supports 4 important quantization types/formats.

Quantization	Data Type	Description	Used For
LLM.int8()	INT8 (8-bit Integer)	Dynamically keeps outliers in higher precision for better accuracy	Inference
FP4	4-bit Floating Point	Standard 4-bit floating-point quantization	QLoRA Training & Inference
NF4	NormalFloat4	Special 4-bit format designed for normally distributed neural network weights	Recommended for QLoRA
Double Quantization	Nested Quantization	Quantizes the quantization constants themselves to save additional memory	Optional optimization for QLoRA
1. INT8 (LLM.int8)

Uses 8 bits to store each weight.

BitsAndBytesConfig(
    load_in_8bit=True
)

Advantages

Better accuracy than 4-bit
Faster inference
About 50% memory reduction
2. FP4 (4-bit Floating Point)

Uses 4-bit floating-point numbers.

BitsAndBytesConfig(
    load_in_4bit=True,
    bnb_4bit_quant_type="fp4"
)

Good when:

Fine-tuning
Smaller models
Less GPU memory
3. NF4 (NormalFloat4)

NF4 is a special 4-bit floating-point format created specifically for neural network weights.

BitsAndBytesConfig(
    load_in_4bit=True,
    bnb_4bit_quant_type="nf4"
)

Why use NF4?

Better accuracy than FP4
Designed for normally distributed weights
Recommended for QLoRA

Hugging Face recommends NF4 for most 4-bit fine-tuning workloads.

4. Double Quantization

This is not another data type. It is an optimization.

BitsAndBytesConfig(
    load_in_4bit=True,
    bnb_4bit_quant_type="nf4",
    bnb_4bit_use_double_quant=True
)

It further reduces GPU memory usage by quantizing the scaling constants used in 4-bit quantization.

Compute Data Types

When using 4-bit quantization, computation can still happen in higher precision. You choose this with:

bnb_4bit_compute_dtype=torch.float16

or

bnb_4bit_compute_dtype=torch.bfloat16

Common compute dtypes are:

torch.float16 (FP16)
torch.bfloat16 (BF16)
torch.float32 (FP32) (less common due to higher memory use)

These are compute data types, not quantization formats.

Summary
Type	Bits	Purpose
INT8	8	Inference
FP4	4	4-bit quantization
NF4	4	Recommended 4-bit quantization for QLoRA
Double Quantization	—	Memory optimization (not a data type)

If you're preparing for GenAI/LLM interviews, it's worth learning why NF4 performs better than FP4 and understanding INT4 vs INT8 vs FP8 quantization, since these are common interview topics.





