Your English can be improved to:

Will the accuracy drop after quantization? Can you show me real benchmarks where this has been tested?

This is an excellent interview question because many people think quantization always hurts accuracy. The answer is: it depends on the number of bits used and the quantization method.

Does quantization reduce accuracy?

Yes—but not always by a noticeable amount.

Quantization	Accuracy Impact	Typical Memory Saving
FP16 → INT8	Almost no loss (usually <1%)	~50%
FP16 → NF4 (4-bit)	Small loss (typically 1–3%)	~75%
FP16 → 3-bit	Moderate loss	~80%
FP16 → 2-bit	Large loss	~87%

These are typical ranges. The exact impact depends on the model and the task.

Benchmark 1 — LLM.int8 Paper (Tim Dettmers, 2022)

This is the paper that introduced the LLM.int8() algorithm used in BitsAndBytes.

Result

The authors evaluated models such as GPT, OPT, BLOOM, and others on language-model benchmarks.

Finding:

INT8 inference produced performance that was essentially identical to FP16 while reducing memory usage by about 50%. The reason is that LLM.int8() detects "outlier" values and processes them in FP16 instead of forcing everything into INT8.

Simple visualization
Model	FP16	INT8
Accuracy	100%	≈99.9–100%
Memory	100%	~50%

This is why 8-bit quantization is widely used in production inference.

Benchmark 2 — QLoRA (NF4)

The QLoRA paper introduced NF4, which BitsAndBytes uses for 4-bit quantization.

The authors fine-tuned LLaMA models in 4-bit and compared them with normal 16-bit fine-tuning.

Their main result

They reported that:

4-bit NF4 + LoRA matched the task performance of 16-bit fine-tuning
A 65B model could be fine-tuned on a single 48 GB GPU.
Their Guanaco models reached 99.3% of ChatGPT's score on the Vicuna benchmark.
Simple comparison
Method	Relative Performance
FP16 Fine-tuning	100%
QLoRA (4-bit NF4)	≈100% (very close)

This result surprised many researchers because they expected a much larger accuracy drop.

Why doesn't accuracy drop much?

Suppose a weight is:

0.742391

After quantization:

0.74

The difference is tiny.

Across billions of parameters, most weights can tolerate these small changes.

Modern quantization methods also preserve the most important values using techniques like:

Per-channel scaling
Outlier detection (LLM.int8)
NF4 codebooks optimized for normally distributed weights
Double quantization for efficient storage
When does accuracy actually drop?

You will start seeing noticeable degradation when:

Using 2-bit or 3-bit quantization.
Quantizing activations as well as weights.
Applying naive integer quantization without handling outliers.
Quantizing very small models that already have limited capacity.

For example:

Precision	Expected Impact
FP16	Best accuracy
INT8	Almost identical
NF4	Slight drop on some tasks
INT4	More noticeable
INT2	Significant degradation
Why do companies still use quantization?

Because the trade-off is often excellent.

Imagine a 7B model:

Precision	GPU Memory
FP16	~14 GB
INT8	~7 GB
NF4	~3.5–4 GB

If the model loses only a small amount of accuracy but uses one quarter of the memory, many production systems consider that a worthwhile trade-off.

Interview answer (30 seconds)

"Yes, quantization can reduce accuracy because model weights are stored with fewer bits. However, modern techniques such as LLM.int8() and NF4 are designed to minimize that loss. The LLM.int8 paper showed almost no degradation compared to FP16 inference, while the QLoRA paper demonstrated that 4-bit NF4 fine-tuning can achieve performance very close to full 16-bit fine-tuning while reducing memory usage dramatically. The lower the bit-width, the greater the risk of accuracy degradation."



































